\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT:BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In New York Seen Slowing Yen's Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par 19:35\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in New York \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading Thursday will likely\par slow - but not reverse - the dollar's rise against the yen.\par    That was the reaction among market participants to the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , carried out through the Federal Reserve Bank\par of New York, at mid-morning, causing the dollar to plummet\par over four yen from its intraday high of Y133.59, hit at 1259\par GMT.\par    Early afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading at\par Y130.37, down from Y131.33 late Wednesday in New York but up\par from its intraday low of Y129.54 hit at 1408 GMT. The dollar\par was trading at DEM1.8210, down from DEM1.8145 late\par Wednesday.\par    Timing was key. In a thin market, ahead of Easter,\par market participants were caught off guard, giving the Fed a\par big bang for its buck. Finance ministers of the\par Group-of-Seven industrialized nations meet in Washington\par next week, and the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par over the weekend, leaving dollar bears hemmed in.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    At around 1315 GMT, the Fed, acting as an agent for the\par BOJ, sold as many as $500 million dollars for yen through\par the Electronic Broking System, a screen-based transaction\par system that has only come into its own in the last few years\par (related story on page 1149). Selling through the EBS allows\par the Fed to broadcast its presence to the financial community\par - forcing those who are long the dollar to sell those\par positions or loose their shirts.\par    Japanese officials confirmed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  took\par "decisive action" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to support the\par yen in an effort to curb rapidly rising trade surpluses.\par    A BOJ spokesman in New York said, "In the New York\par market the Fed made action on behalf of the BOJ."\par    The selling was accompanied by a vigorous verbal\par endorsement of the action by Treasury Secretary Robert\par Rubin.\par    "We share the concern expressed by the Japanese prime\par minister about recent weakness in the yen, and in that\par context we welcome the action undertaken by the Japanese\par authorities in the exchange market to support the value of\par the yen," Rubin said in a statement (related story page\par 1147).\par    In all, the dollar has lost around 3.7% of its value\par against the yen this week, after grinding lower the first\par three days of this week on talk that Prime Minister Ryutaro\par Hashimoto would unveil significant tax cuts Thrusday.\par    The roughly Y4 trillion in tax cuts the Japanese prime\par minister unveiled didn't please the market, however, and the\par yen began to weaken further, leaving little between the\par dollar and six and 1/2 year highs of Y135.42 it hit last\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  04-09-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1335EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1148 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du4900wwf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg