\par\par \b \uc2  REPEAT:BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  In New York Seen Slowing Yen's Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par 19:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Thursday will likely slow - but not reverse - the dollar's rise against the yen. \par \par \uc2  That was the reaction among market participants to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , carried out through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, at mid-morning, causing the dollar to plummet over four yen from its intraday high of Y133.59, hit at 1259 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Early afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading at Y130.37, down from Y131.33 late Wednesday in New York but up from its intraday low of Y129.54 hit at 1408 GMT. The dollar was trading at DEM1.8210, down from DEM1.8145 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Timing was key. In a thin market, ahead of Easter, market participants were caught off guard, giving the Fed a big bang for its buck. Finance ministers of the Group-of-Seven industrialized nations meet in Washington next week, and the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the weekend, leaving dollar bears hemmed in. \par \par \uc2  At around 1315 GMT, the Fed, acting as an agent for the BOJ, sold as many as $500 million dollars for yen through the Electronic Broking System, a screen-based transaction system that has only come into its own in the last few years (related story on page 1149). Selling through the EBS allows the Fed to broadcast its presence to the financial community - forcing those who are long the dollar to sell those positions or loose their shirts. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took 'decisive action' in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen in an effort to curb rapidly rising trade surpluses. \par \par \uc2  A BOJ spokesman in New York said, 'In the New York market the Fed made action on behalf of the BOJ.' \par \par \uc2  The selling was accompanied by a vigorous verbal endorsement of the action by Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin. \par \par \uc2  'We share the concern expressed by the Japanese prime minister about recent weakness in the yen, and in that context we welcome the action undertaken by the Japanese authorities in the exchange market to support the value of the yen,' Rubin said in a statement (related story page 1147). \par \par \uc2  In all, the dollar has lost around 3.7% of its value against the yen this week, after grinding lower the first three days of this week on talk that Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto would unveil significant tax cuts Thrusday. \par \par \uc2  The roughly Y4 trillion in tax cuts the Japanese prime minister unveiled didn't please the market, however, and the yen began to weaken further, leaving little between the dollar and six and 1/2 year highs of Y135.42 it hit last week. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 09-04-98 \par \par \uc2  1736GMT \par \par \uc2  1148 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020010916du4900k7s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
