\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-BoJ's lifeline to yen puts spotlight on G7. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Svea Herbst-Bayliss \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 9 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan threw a lifeline to its ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, helping refocus market attention on next week's Group of Seven meeting where the yen's future is likely to be a key topic. \par \par \uc2  Swooping into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as European dealers prepared for a four-day holiday weekend and New York traders anticipated a dull day, the Bank of Japan sold dollars for yen through the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In just over an hour, the dollar tumbled four yen Thursday morning to a 129.52-yen session low, nearly three percent below its New York open, before settling into sideways trade around 130 yen in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were stunned, having just discounted the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because trading was thin before the Easter holiday weekend and the dollar's overnight rise would seem to make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less effective. \par \par \uc2  Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar had climbed as markets had signaled thumbs-down to Tokyo's latest stimulus plan - which promised 10 trillion yen in spending including four trillion yen in tax cuts. One trader said looked as if "Japan is just tossing around big numbers." \par \par \uc2  But analysts termed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 's timing as shrewd, noting the BoJ successfully fired a warning shot and left markets unlikely to bid the dollar much higher next week. \par \par \uc2  "There is definitely caution now and this should keep dollar/yen confined to a trading range somewhere between 128 and 133" early next week, said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group Ltd. \par \par \uc2  A key reason for stepping into a rising dollar's way may have been the fear that any yen weakness could snowball, which could send Tokyo stocks tumbling and possibly touch off a fresh Asian financial crisis, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Maybe Japanese officials were disappointed with how the market reacted to the package and this was Plan B," said Henry Willmore, senior economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  If the dollar were to climb to 135 or 140 yen, the market might believe a push to 150 yen is also within reach, he added. \par \par \uc2  Bearing this in mind, analysts said the Bank of Japan would probably not hesitate to sell dollars again at the first sign of revived yen weakness, especially since the bank has ample reserves to spend on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There is a pretty substantial risk of more Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Willmore said. "While they can get a bigger move for the amount of money in a thin market market, they may want to do it in a situation where people are taking a more aggressive position," \par \par \uc2  But Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone may not make a lasting impression, analysts agreed, adding further reaction from Group of Seven nations, gathering in Washington for a meeting next week, would be a key factor. \par \par \uc2  "A lot will depend on how the G7 read the Japanese efforts on taxes and whether there will be more stimulus. In fact U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin's support may suggest the G7 may be trying to establish a lower dollar/yen range, closer to 125 yen," Refco's Malpede said. \par \par \uc2  Therefore, "the markets will watch the G7 communique very carefully," he said. \par \par \uc2  As G7 leaders applauded Japan's stimulus efforts Thursday, dealers at major U.S.-based banks noticed the other G7 nations were absent from Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "What reason would the U.S. or Germany have to step in now? The U.S. economy is still strong and the Bundesbank seems to feel pretty comfortable with exchange rates," said one analyst, asking not to be named. \par \par \uc2  With the G7 likely to hold off on committing money to Japan before seeing Tokyo help itself, a dollar/yen rebound could be on the horizon, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Given the circumstances, we recently raised our forecast for dollar/yen in the next 12 months to 150 from 140," Barclays' Willmore said. While that forecast was made before Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Willmore said he feels the dollar is still headed higher. \par \par \uc2  ((-North American Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4907mx6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
