\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar surges, then tumbles as  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chris Stetkiewicz \par \uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par 21:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, April 9 (Reuters) - The dollar ended slightly lower against the Japanese yen Thursday after a roller-coaster session that saw the Bank of Japan enter the market to bolster the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had initially risen sharply overnight after investors rejected Japan's latest and most-aggressive proposals to date to stimulate its staggering economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose somewhat against the German mark, which also suffered heavy losses against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto in Tokyo announced tax cuts of four trillion yen ($30 billion) and said spending programs would boost the size of a new stimulus package to 10 trillion yen ($75 billion). \par \par \uc2  But the dollar rose on the news as investors interpreted the proposals as too timid to revive Japan's economy. \par \par \uc2  Japanese business leaders and financial economists questioned the permanence of the tax cuts and also fretted that conservative Japanese consumers might just save the money rather than spend it. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rose more than two yen to as high as 133.62 yen in early trading. \par \par \uc2  But shortly after the start of New York trading, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers spotted the Bank of Japan, working through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, buying yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar began to slide lower, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  each confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In Washington, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin - an influential voice in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - praised Japan's action, saying he shared Japan's concern over the weakened yen. \par \par \uc2  In just over an hour, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell more than four yen to 129.52 yen. \par \par \uc2  But late in the session the dollar climbed off its lows, aided by a statement from an unidentified U.S. Treasury official saying that U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy had not changed. \par \par \uc2  Some market participants had begun to wonder if the Treasury was shifting away from a longstanding public stance supporting a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended lower at 131.20 yen versus 131.37 at Wednesday's close. The dollar rose to 1.8225 marks from 1.8135 marks. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the pound fell to $1.6697 from $1.6755. The dollar rose to 1.5155 Swiss francs from 1.5077 Swiss francs. The U.S. unit was also higher at Canadian $1.4265 from Canadian $1.4244. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4907mww\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
