\par\par \b \uc2  Rubin welcomes  \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1998\par 15:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON, April 9 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin on Thursday welcomed Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster the yen's value but made no mention whether the United States was joining the action. \par \par \uc2  "We share the concern expressed by the Japanese Prime Minister (Hashimoto) about recent weakness in the yen, and in that context we welcome the action taken by the Japanese authorities in the exchange market to support the value of the yen," Rubin said in a statement issued at the Treasury Department. \par \par \uc2  Rubin's statement came as the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in Tokyo it had bought yen and sold dollars to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has fallen recently on doubts over a weak economy. \par \par \uc2  Rubin also gave a cautious welcome to an overnight announcement in Tokyo of long-awaited steps to boost Japan's ailing economy. Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto announced four trillion yen ($30.5 billion) in income tax cuts and 10 trillion yen ($76.3 billion) in extra spending. \par \par \uc2  "We look forward to seeing the details later this month," Rubin said, adding "What is crucial is that Japan move quickly to put in place a strong program." \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's move to boost the yen came less than a week ahead of a meeting in Washington of finance ministers from the Group of Seven top industrial nations, at which Japan's economy is expected to take center stage. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4907mk9\par }\page {