\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  vols stay subdued as spot drifts. \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, April 7 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for dollar-pair options remained subdued, particularly in the shorter maturities, in lackluster U.S. trading ahead of a long weekend, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The spot dollar was steadily retreating from last week's highs above 1.8550 marks and 135 yen, also putting under duress a lot of long positions in short-dated upside strike prices bought at rich premium levels last week, they added. \par \par \uc2  "With the holidays coming up at the end of the week, it's just been really lackluster," said John Foreman, a senior analyst at Technical Data. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. market will be closed on Friday for Good Friday, while much of Europe awaits a four-day Easter weekend. Spot dollar/mark, which had rallied sharply early last week, now appeared to have lost steam, drifting near 1.84 marks. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark implied volatility stood at 7.8/8.0 percent, off 0.1-0.2 point from late Monday levels. The back end was little changed with one-year at 9.3/9.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen activity was also likely to be subdued ahead of a Group of Seven meeting next week, although the dollar's pullback to a low of 133.31 yen today that could extend a little further, was seen to be a temporary correction. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Golden Week holidays, from late April to early May, were also pressuring volatilities in the one-month area, traders added. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen was down about 0.3 point from Monday at 12.4/12.6 percent, while one-year was steady at 13.2/13.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's pullback had largely been expected after last week's seven-yen rally and that downside interest appeared limited to short-term protection. \par \par \uc2  "There aren't many people calling for selling dollars for a lot lower levels," said a trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  Risk reversals, the put/call price differential, slightly favored the downside strikes in the very short dates, which have lately been used mainly to protect long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  But risk reversals in the one-month area were around par and the back months continued to favor yen puts, reflecting the market's long-term dollar bullishness. \par \par \uc2  The market showed little immediate reaction to Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami's remarks overnight that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have only a limited impact unless the market's mood changed. \par \par \uc2  But traders and analysts said the reaction seemed muted mainly because the dollar had been consolidative on its own this week. \par \par \uc2  "If we start getting back up to 135 yen, people will be a lot more willing to buy (dollars) without fear that they could get jumped on by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Foreman said. "But I think down here it's not really a major consideration." ((Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4707ue7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
