\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen bears back off, but not very far. \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 7 (Reuters) - The dollar shed some of its recent gains against the yen on Tuesday as the market began to square off positions ahead of next week's G7 finance ministers' meeting and the long Easter weekend. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar/yen rally still had further to go, traders and analysts said, citing the stark contrast between U.S. and Japanese economic fundamentals, and the apparent benign neglect on the part of the Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar got some support in European trade when Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaru Hayami said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have only a limited impact unless the market's mood changed. \par \par \uc2  "But dollar/yen's quite top-heavy because people want to adjust positions ahead of the G7 after buying dollar/yen last week when they panicked on the Moody's news about Japan," said one trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Still, there's no reason to buy yen aggressively, so the strategy right now seems to be to buy dollars on dips." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 133.65/70 yen at 1450 GMT, down from its late Monday European level of 134.62/67. This was about 1.4 percent lower than the 6-1/2 year high last Friday when Moody's said it was changing the outlook on Japan's triple-A sovereign debt to "negative" from "stable". \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they did not expect dollar/yen to burst into new territory ahead of next week's gathering of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on the sidelines of the semi-annual IMF/World Bank meeting. \par \par \uc2  There was also attention on what economic measures the Japanese government would reveal once the national budget for 1998/99 is enacted. The budget is expected to pass the Upper House of parliament on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "Tax cuts are obviously where the key still lies, and we're just going to have to wait for the budget and what happens after that," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury analyst at NatWest. \par \par \uc2  "But it's a very brave person who's willing to pick a top in dollar/yen at the moment. The market has been surprised by the way they (the authorities) have been so neglectful," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some doubted whether substantial Japanese tax cuts - something which senior officials at home and abroad have been clamouring for - would have a lasting impact on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "If people get more money in their pay packets, they'll save instead of spend...Longer-term, dollar/yen will go to 140. The deflationary problem - too little money chasing too many goods - is not going away," said Nick Shamim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at ANZ Investment. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark, meanwhile, was slightly softer but bound in a tight range amid a mixed series of German data. It was quoted at 1.8396/06 marks against 1.8421/26 late on Monday. \par \par \uc2  German unemployment data showed a marked improvement, but dollar/mark showed little reaction as it was in line with figures leaked over the weekend. Jobless numbers fell to 4.623 million in March from 4.819 million the previous month, while the unemployment rate fell to 12.1 percent from February's 12.6. \par \par \uc2  However, February's industrial output was worse than expected, falling 1.2 percent on the month against forecasts of a 0.6 percent drop. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was awaiting the Bank of England's monetary policy meeting which will take place on Wednesday and Thursday, though most in the market did not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change the base rate from its current 7.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The pound was quoted at 3.0675/85 marks, down about 1.4 percent from its 8-1/2 year high of 3.1085 reached a week ago, and against 3.0643/48 seen in late European trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, sterling was at $1.6660/70 against Monday's $1.6622/32. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4707u7d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
