\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr/mark soft on reports of fall in German jobless. \b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 6 (Reuters) - Dollar/mark fell in early Europe on Monday on weekend newspaper reports of an improvement in German unemployment, while dollar/yen was easier, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Leaks on German unemployment pushed dollar/mark down this morning," said Paul Bednarczyk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at 4CAST. \par \par \uc2  "There's some profit-taking going on in dollar/yen and fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may limit activity." \par \par \uc2  A report by the newspaper Welt am Sonntag of a 200,000 fall in German unemployment in March and a seasonally unadjusted fall in the jobless rate to 12.1 percent from 12.6 percent in February drove dollar/mark to 1.8400 in early trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  German unemployment figures are due to be released by the Federal Labour office at 0755 GMT on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  At 0720 GMT the dollar was at 1.8426/31 marks and 134.85/80 yen versus 1.8485/95 marks and 134.89/99 yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Another report from a British newspaper that the presidency of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to go to head of the European monetary institute Wim Duisenberg may support the mark against the dollar, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "It's potentially good, removing some uncertainty and could be a catalyst for the mark to recover against the dollar," said Marco Pianelli, senior economist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  "If there was a statement from France backing Duisenberg, it would be even better," Nomura's Pianelli said. \par \par \uc2  Last year France nominated Bank of France governor Jean Claude Trichet for the presidency of the ECB. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the relative strength of the mark and the yen versus the dollar can be seen in the cross rate, hovering near three-month highs. \par \par \uc2  At 0745 GMT mark/yen was at 73.11/14, slightly firmer from Friday's levels around 72.98/73.01. \par \par \uc2  The yen may hold firm on Monday, but analysts predict a higher dollar by the end of this week. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult for the market to believe all the talk of stimulus and tax cuts. People need something more concrete to go on," said 4CAST's Bednarczyk. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto admitted in parliament that the economy faces its toughest problems in half a century and vowed to make a quick and bold response. He also denied the economy was on the verge of a collapse. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere there was little impact on the Swiss franc from comments by Swiss National Bank Board member Bruno Gehrig who said the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not pose a threat to Switzerlnd's ongoing economic revival. \par \par \uc2  At 0815 GMT, the mark was trading at 82.66/68 Swiss centimes versus 82.60/63 Swiss centimes late on Friday, dollar/Swiss franc was 1.5230/40 from 1.5271/81. Over the past few days, the Swiss franc appears to have lost its safe-haven allure, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There's no yield in Swiss francs either," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Sterling, normally a high-yielding safe-haven, also lost some ground in early trade after comments by Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown. \par \par \uc2  Brown said sterling strength was a problem for exporters, but ruled out short-term measures that would deflect from his goal of long-term economic stability. \par \par \uc2  But a Reuters survey of economists shows there may have been an improvement in the manufacturing sector over the past month. \par \par \uc2  March industrial production is forecast to rise 0.2 percent month-on-month and rise 0.1 percent year-on-year. Manufacturing output is seen growing by 0.2 percent from February and is forecast to remain unchanged from February 1997. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du46083zt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
