\par\par \b \uc2  Japan markets shudder as Moody's joins pessimists. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Edmund Klamann \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1998\par 06:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 3 (Reuters) - Japan's stocks, bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  all tumbled on Friday, after influential U.S. credit rating agency Moody's Investors Service joined a growing chorus of doomsayers on the country's ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  Moody's lowered its outlook on Japan's sovereign debt rating to negative from stable, citing the nation's worsening fiscal situation and Japanese leaders' inability to hammer out policies that could reverse a seven-year economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  Although the ratings warning maintained Japan's Aaa status, it roiled markets that were already on the defensive after a closely watched \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey the day before showed a sharp drop in business sentiment across the economy. \par \par \uc2  During the day, the Moody's news pushed the yen to its lowest level against the dollar in 6-= years and knocked a full point off the price of benchmark Japanese government bond futures, while the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average of 225 issues reversed course from an early rally. \par \par \uc2  "Stocks were sold in a panic but they soon stabilised," Okasan Securities strategist Akihiro Naemura said. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said Moody's announcement would not have a negative impact directly on the stock market but its impact on the yen and bonds could affect stocks. \par \par \uc2  "The announcement itself will not continue to be a major negative factor as it only confirmed the current poor state of the Japanese economy," said Nikko Securities general manager Yasuo Ueki. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average ended at 15,517.78, down 1.18 percent. The stock benchmark had fallen as low as 15,465.13 after the Moody's announcement. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark June futures contract for Japanese government bonds ended down 0.80 at 129.27, managing to claw its way back from a low of 128.90. \par \par \uc2  After the announcement by Moody's pounded the yen to its lowest level since September 9, 1991, the Bank of Japan appeared to have moved into action. Bankers said they thought the BOJ made its long-awaited market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  "The problems about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Moody's pointed out are not new to us. But there is a risk scenario that the weak yen may spill over to other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yuji Matsuura, chief dealer at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Group at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank. \par \par \uc2  In late Tokyo trade, the dollar was being quoted at 134.63/73 yen, after having risen earlier to 135 yen flat. \par \par \uc2  The Moody's report stressed that the negative outlook merely signified "the identification of developments that may lead to a formal review of the rating over an 18-month to two-year horizon". \par \par \uc2  But in an interview with Reuters Financial Television, Vincent Truglia, a New York-based managing director for Moody's, said that underlying risks had increased for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "As everyone knows we are now in a recession by any measurement and there is quite a bit of concern that the recession might deepen," he said. "The present environment is much, much more difficult than the Japanese authorities have had to face for many, many years." \par \par \uc2  The government's fiscal bind was evident in policy wrangling over whether to implement massive and permanent tax cuts in an effort to resuscitate the economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Hikaru Matsunaga told a parliamentary committee on Friday that the issue "has to be considered very carefully", since any tax cuts would have to be paid for by future generations. \par \par \uc2  Some policymakers indicated they finally had a sense of urgency about the economy's woes. \par \par \uc2  "We will deal with the current state of the economy with a sense of crisis," Liberal Democratic Party secretary-general Koichi Kato was quoted as telling party officials. ((Tokyo Equities Desk +81 3 3432-9052 tokyo.equities.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du43084xf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
