\par\par \b \uc2  Japan economic saga to dominate markets next week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Claudia Parsons \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1998\par 17:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 3 (Reuters) - Japanese economic woes will continue to take centre stage in financial markets in the week ahead, though the Bank of England's (BoE) monetary policy meeting will also be closely watched, economists said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  With no market-moving data due out of the United States, the driving force would be the continuing saga of Japan's efforts to boost its economy, with particular focus on any tax measures. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar/yen soared to a 6-1/2 year peak of 135.20 early on Friday on news Moody's Investors Service cut its outlook for Japan's sovereign debt to negative from stable. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they had seen the Bank of Japan sell dollars on electronic broking systems overnight as the yen pared some of its losses. \par \par \uc2  "This whole concern about Japan is likely to dominate everything," said Rob Hayward, international economist at BankAmerica in London. \par \par \uc2  Dick Howard, senior economist at Julius Baer Investments, said the yen could be supported by further BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming week. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese may be content just to draw a line and try to stop the yen falling further - they may not want to try to push it back up to 125," Howard said. \par \par \uc2  Also under the spotlight next week will be any fund flows out of Japanese assets into other markets amid domestic gloom heightened by a dismal reading in Thursday's Tankan survey of corporate sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The BoE's Monetary Policy Committee holds a monthly two-day meeting beginning on Wednesday to set interest rates, though most analysts did not expect a rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "Since the last meeting, we've had the downward revision to average earnings, we've had continued strength of sterling, and we've had all the survey evidence suggesting retailing is running into trouble," said Julian Jessop, chief European economist at Nikko Europe. \par \par \uc2  Sterling could climb higher against the mark ahead of the MPC decision, expected on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't think anyone would like to be short of sterling going into the meeting just in case they do raise rates," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  "This week's economic data have shown that despite what was heard from manufacturers it's not clear that the economy is slowing sufficiently to prevent inflation picking up," he added. \par \par \uc2  Another highlight of the week is German unemployment and industrial production data which analysts said could provide fresh guidelines on when the Bundesbank might rates its rates to facilitate European convergence toward a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Germany's March unemployment figures are due on Tuesday while February industrial production will be released on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But Julius Baer's Howard played down the significance of the German data next week. \par \par \uc2  "I'd say there's no way on domestic grounds the Germans would be thinking about raising interest rates at the present moment," he said. \par \par \uc2  "So the issue is whether they make a euro-inspired token interest rate increase in May, and I suspect that's a bit doubtful," he added. \par \par \uc2  Investors will be on the lookout for any comments on the Japanese economy and the yen from the Group of 10 central bankers who hold their regular monthly meeting in Basle on Monday. \par \par \uc2  BankAmerica's Hayward said any hints about the U.S. stance on the yen weakness prior to the Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers' meeting on April 15 could also be crucial for markets. \par \par \uc2  "What the market wants to see is some support from the (U.S.) Fed, that's why the G7 meeting will become more important," said Hayward. \par \par \uc2  But the market's perception was that the U.S. administration would be reluctant to take part in any yen supportive action, he said. \par \par \uc2  Denmark faces the Supreme Court's verdict on Monday in a case testing the legality of the country's membership of the European Union. \par \par \uc2  The European Monetary Institute holds its council meeting in Frankfurt on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Other data include UK industrial production for February on Monday, Italian March consumer prices on Wednesday and U.S. March producer prices on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du430825o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
