\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dlr/yen pushed to 6-1/2 yr highs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pratima Desai \par \uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 3 (Reuters) - A dollar/yen bounce to six and a half year highs dominated trade in Europe on Friday as the growing fragility of Japan's economy and financial system were highlighted after Moody's lowered its outlook, traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw the dollar pull back below 135 yen in early trade, but analysts remain convinced of a dollar/yen move into new territory in the near-term. \par \par \uc2  "It was a Moody's story today," said Dick Howard, director of economic research at Julius Baer Investments. "The new fiscal year (April 1) will see capital outflows from Japan." \par \par \uc2  The end of capital repatriation on March 31 was a cue for Japanese investors to push funds into high-yielding assets. \par \par \uc2  At 1545 GMT dollar/yen was trading at 134.73/83 after briefly touching a new six and a half year high at 135.42 and a morning peak at 135.10 and versus 133.43/53 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark was at 1.8480/90 versus an early high at 1.8557 and versus 1.8485/95 late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Moody's cut the rating outlook for both Japan's country ceiling and the government's domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to negative from stable. Later it lowered the long-term debt rating outlook for government backed bodies to negative from stable. \par \par \uc2  Ian Harwood, global economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson, said the 36,000 drop in non-farm payrolls versus a forecast gain of 220,000 would have a limited impact on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Wall Street (the U.S. stock market) will like the data and support the dollar," he said. "As far as the yen is concerned the situation is bleak. We've had nothing but bad news out of Japan and it's clear the economy is going into a recession." \par \par \uc2  February non-farm payrolls were revised to an increase of 252,000 from 310,000. The March jobless rate rose to 4.7 percent from 4.6 percent in February, but analysts said the jobs data did not square with other strong data over the past month. \par \par \uc2  "There is a risk that the Japanese government sits on its hands and does nothing," Harwood at Dresdner said. \par \par \uc2  If Japan's government does nothing substantial to stimulate the economy, analysts believe the yen's decline versus other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue. \par \par \uc2  "But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will smooth the drop and provide selling opportunities," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  James Shugg, economist at Westpac said there was a risk dollar/yen could see 150. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen around 135 increases the chances of it seeing 150. If we see 138 next week the chances go up," he said. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts, including Shugg at Westpac predict, the Japanese government will eventually have to drop their indecisiveness and take a stance to pump cash and confidence into the economy. \par \par \uc2  "Eventually a new package will deliver around 10 trillion worth of tax cuts," Shugg said. \par \par \uc2  Following the pattern set by the yen, the mark was easier versus the dollar for most of the European trading session. \par \par \uc2  The German Reuters/BME services business activity seasonally adjusted index rose to 55.30 in March from 55.23 in February but had little impact on mark levels. \par \par \uc2  Britain's service sector also showed strong growth in March, but at a marginally lower rate. The business activity index was at 60.3 in March from 60.4 in February. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the report had little impact on sterling levels as it added little to the arguments either for higher or lower interest rates. The Bank of England's monetary policy committee meets next Thursday and Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 1515 GMT sterling/mark was at 3.0671/78 versus 3.0880/90 late Thursday and cable was at $1.6583/90 versus $1.668/90. \par \par \uc2  ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 2737, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du43081fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
