\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Japan MOF Jawboning Caps Dlr Rise Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 1998\par 07:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--After renewing its three-month high against the yen, the dollar was dragged down by Japanese officials warning against its recent quick rise, leaving it slightly higher Wednesday than its levels in New York late Tuesday. The dollar, meanwhile, is flat against the Deutsche mark. \par \par \uc2  The first day of Japan's 'Big Bang' financial deregulation - a series of financial-market reforms involving liberalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and securities transactions - didn't bode well for the Tokyo stock market. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei stock average fell sharply from the start of Wednesday trading, to as low as 16,147.75 points from Tuesday's close of 16,527.17, which in turn drove overseas hedge funds and Japanese investors to buy the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose quickly to Y133.85 at 0024 GMT (7:24 p.m. EST Tuesday), the highest since Jan. 7, when it traded at a five-year high of Y134.40, but suddenly reversed its course on remarks from senior officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, the influential vice finance minister for international affairs, said the yen's weakness had 'gone too far,' adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues would be discussed at the next meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialized countries coming up in two weeks. \par \par \uc2  That was followed by similar warnings by two more ministry officials, invoking fears of dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting U.S. investment funds and Japanese institutional investors to sell dollars to book profits, traders said. \par \par \uc2  'The comments provided a good excuse to unwind dollar-long positions,' said Kazuhito Furukawa, a senior trader at Nippon Credit Bank. 'Some big economic data are scheduled to be released later this week both in the U.S. and Japan, so many traders wanted to square positions.' \par \par \uc2  Around 0540 GMT (12:40 a.m. EST), the dollar is quoted at Y133.35, slightly above Y133.22 late Tuesday in New York and up from Y133.23 late Tuesday in London. It also is at DEM1.8485, unchanged from late Tuesday in New York and higher than DEM1.8475 in London Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 01-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0554GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 28038 61027 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4102rjo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
