\par\par \b \uc2  Stronger dollar builds momentum in Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Mar 31 (Reuters) - A strong dollar pushed to fresh highs across the board in Europe on Tuesday as the market discounted an expected exodus of capital out of Japan, highlighting a general flight from low yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar pushed to a fresh 2-1/2-month high against the yen, a seven-month peak against the mark and a 7-1/2-month high against the Swiss franc in morning European trade. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar upside is due mainly to the Japanese situation, but there is also interest rate expectations in that the U.S. interest rate differential is not going to narrow," said David Brickman, international economist at PaineWebber in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said expectations of the so-called seasonal 'wall of money' flow of investment out of Japan at the start of the new fiscal year on April 1 has been exaggerated this year as it coincides with financial market deregulation in Japan. \par \par \uc2  This is seen making April's outflow of Japanese capital larger than ever and, combined with Japan's moribund economy, is likely to see further upside in dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  "Historically, those who have argued for these seasonal patterns have been disappointed, but this is an unusual fiscal year-end," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen rose to a peak of 133.70 in morning trade, its highest level since January 7. By 1145 GMT, it edged back from those highs but was still firm at 133.00/10 yen and well above late Monday's European level of 131.91/2.01 yen. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers were wary of taking dollar/yen too far, too fast, and that it would remain to be see how big the capital outflow from Japan would actually be. \par \par \uc2  "The attention is on fresh investment out of Japan, but this story is beginning to look a bit distorted," said one U.S. bank dealer. "There certainly has been fresh investment flows, but so far this hasn't been that huge." \par \par \uc2  The market remains wary of possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen weakness, but the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action as the fiscal year-end drew to a close today is leaving the market more confident in pushing the dollar higher. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese authorities have been dropping huge hints threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but until they back it up with action the yen is a one-way bet at the moment," said Lee Ferridge, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  Although Japan's economic outlook remains grim and Thursday's quarterly Tankan sentiment survey is expected to be weak, dealers said much of this has been discounted. \par \par \uc2  But this is unlikely to provide a strong brake for dollar/yen as analysts note renewed interest for high-yielding carry trades funded by borrowing in low-yielding yen and Swiss francs, adding to pressure on those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This pressure helped the Swiss franc to a 7-1/2 month low of 1.5275 per dollar in earlier trade. By midday Europe, it settled back to 1.5208/18 but was still above Monday's 1.5152/62 francs. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts were also mindful that Swiss franc weakness comes amid growing comfort with the onset of Europe's economic and monetary union in 1999, taking away some of the Swiss franc's safe-haven status. \par \par \uc2  Although few expect the United States to move on interest rates at today's policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee meeting, interest rate differentials are likely to continue to favour the United States against Europe and Japan. \par \par \uc2  The market bias is for an eventual tightening of U.S. rates later this year. This will continue to provide dollar/mark support against an outlook for low, steady rates in Germany. \par \par \uc2  PaineWebber's Brickman doesn't see the need for higher U.S. interest rates at the moment but, at the same time, German and European interest rates are likely to remain low over the next few months. \par \par \uc2  "We do not expect an upward move in European rates in the second quarter, but we could see a rate rise before EMU begins in 1999," said Brickman. \par \par \uc2  "When that becomes evident, that will help turn the tide against the dollar - but that's probably about three to six months down the line." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/mark stepped back from its earlier seven-month high of 1.8540 marks, settling at 1.8462/67 marks at midday Europe against 1.8416/21 late Monday. ((Andrew Reierson, London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3v08co4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
