\par\par \b \uc2  Yen bears in command as Japan gloom deepens. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Reierson \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Mar 30 (Reuters) - Yen bears were in command on Monday as the already sickly Japanese unit struggled under the weight of a growing list of negative factors. \par \par \uc2  Increasing pessimism over Japan's economy overtook waning expectations that the Japanese authorities would take more direct action to shore up its financial and equity markets ahead of Tuesday's fiscal year-end book closings. \par \par \uc2  "Today's weak industrial production data was the final nail in the coffin," said Teis Knuthsen, chief economist at SE Banken in London. \par \par \uc2  Japan industrial production data released overnight showed a sharp 3.3 percent decline in February and Japan's trade ministry said it expected falls of 2.5 percent in both March and April. \par \par \uc2  The gloom in Japan was exacerbated by a near-three percent decline in the benchmark Nikkei stock index to a close of 16,263.04. \par \par \uc2  That means Japanese banks are set to show net unrealised losses on their equity holdings for the fiscal year, as the absence of government "price-keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 " has left the index well below the 18,003 level reached at the end of fiscal 1996/97. \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that yen weakness was also being fuelled by speculation of dollar demand from Japanese banks' before the fiscal year-end as provision for bad loans to Asia. \par \par \uc2  The yen slipped to 2-1/2 month lows against the mark and dollar and a 5-1/2 year low against buoyant sterling in morning European trade on Monday. By 1115 GMT, dollar/yen stood at 131.72/82, just off a session high of 131.98 and well above 130.12/22 seen late Friday in Europe. \par \par \uc2  Mark/yen was trading at its session peak of 71.90/93, above 71.22/25 late Friday, while sterling/yen stood at 221.57/88 from 215.79 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see little respite for the yen after the fiscal year-end with next key focus being Thursday's key Bank of Japan quarterly Tankan business sentiment survey. \par \par \uc2  "As far as the Tankan is concerned, the outlook is almost universally gloomy," said Michael Metcalfe, treasury analyst at NatWest Markets in London. "Japan is stuck in a rut, the key now will be to see when Japanese consumer confidence is restored." \par \par \uc2  He sees dollar/yen at 135 a realistic target but is mindful that even beyond the fiscal year-end, the implied threat of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the yen is likely to remain and keep yen sales from becoming too aggressive. \par \par \uc2  The market eyed warily comments from Japan's Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act appropriately to maintain stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "I would not be surprised by some \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next 24 hours, but the question remains as to how sustainable it could be given the negative fundamental outlook for Japan," said Mark Geddes, treasury economist at ABN-Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  Another likely negative yen factor is seen in the April 1 deregulation of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. This step will see the system of licensed \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks abolished and allow almost anyone to participate in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Although the impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets remains to be seen, analysts said the financial deregulation could see Japanese investment funds flow out of the country as well as put tough competitive pressure on Japan's beleaguered financial sector. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, firmer oil prices amid oil producers' recent efforts to curb output to an oversupplied global market is also a negative yen factor, although minor. \par \par \uc2  "Higher oil prices support oil producers' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Norwegian crown but will be a drag on the yen. Japan is a significant oil importer and higher oil means another bearish factor for the yen," said SE Banken's Knuthsen. \par \par \uc2  However, with so much bad news swirling around the yen, dealers are aware the market may have already absorbed the bulk of negative sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "The market is saturated with bad news about Japan and a lot of that has been discounted," said a U.S. bank dealer. "It may take a while, but the market will be getting to a point where it reacts more to any hint the situation in Japan is not as bad as expected." ((London newsroom +44 171 542 6745, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3u08eb5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
