\par\par \b \uc2  FEATURE-A new money order in Asia-Is it a pipe dream? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 1,147 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 08:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, March 27 (Reuters) - Short-term capital flows, blamed as principal offenders in Asia's financial crisis, will only get more erratic with Japan's "Big Bang". \par \par \uc2  Some say the world needs a new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  order to rein in such "hot money". \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin last month stressed the need to come up with a new architecture for the global financial system in order to avert another crisis. \par \par \uc2  Eisuke Sakakibara, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, said many world leaders may well be starting to contemplate the idea of a financial agreement along the lines of the Bretton Woods system. \par \par \uc2  The world's top financial officials are expected to discuss short-term capital flows at an interim committee meeting of the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank in Washington in April. \par \par \uc2  But monetary sources in Tokyo played down the drive for a new order. \par \par \uc2  "I see neither Washington nor Tokyo seriously working on or taking an organised approach to a new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  structure," a Japanese monetary source said. \par \par \uc2  It is still at the stage of throwing out an abstract idea and watching others respond to it, he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a solution along the lines of Bretton Woods, a postwar \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system based on fixed exchange rates, is difficult in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with a daily turnover of $1.2 trillion. \par \par \uc2  IGNORED BY BIG THREE \par \par \uc2  In July 1994, the Bretton Woods Commission, a high-level independent commission headed by former U.S. Federal Reserve chairman Paul Volcker, said countries should establish a more formal system of coordination to help avoid excessive exchange rate misalignments that lead to wild volatility. \par \par \uc2  But the world's three biggest economies - the United States, Japan and Germany - have turned a deaf ear. \par \par \uc2  Spurred by international criticism that Tokyo is not doing enough for Asia, Japan has came up with various initiatives in the past six months while injecting substantial money into troubled Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  Those Tokyo initiatives, while stopping short of presenting a new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  scheme, spotlighted issues that should be addressed under a new order, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In September, Japan floated the idea of an "Asia Fund" - a region-wide facility to address financial crises. \par \par \uc2  But that quickly ran into opposition from the United States, Europe and the IMF, who feared such a fund would disburse cash without demanding policy adjustments often required by the IMF as a cure for crises. \par \par \uc2  Last November, as the Asian crisis took a turn for the worse, finance ministers of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum met in Manila and agreed on the need for more money. \par \par \uc2  But the idea of a permanent fund was quietly dropped. \par \par \uc2  Deputy finance officials from Asia and the United States are expected to meet on Thursday and Friday in Tokyo to follow up on initiatives of the so-called Manila Framework. Those initiatives include a mechanism for regional surveillance, enhanced economic and technical cooperation, and a cooperative financing arrangement that would supplement the IMF. \par \par \uc2  MORAL HAZARD \par \par \uc2  Japan also floated a rescue concept of asking the World Bank and Asian Development Bank (ADB) to guarantee the debts of troubled Asian nations. \par \par \uc2  Officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said the plan could help restore confidence in the region because a guarantee could solve short-term funding problems. \par \par \uc2  But it failed to win support from the United States, which raised objections over "moral hazard" - the fear that promises of a future bailout would prompt ever riskier lending. \par \par \uc2  A senior MOF official attributed the failures of Japan's ideas to a difference in style. \par \par \uc2  "Our style is to give a blood transfusion to keep a seriously injured person from dying. Meanwhile, the American style is to tell those who are bleeding badly to first try to heal themselves," the senior MOF official said. \par \par \uc2  C.H. Kwan, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute, said: "Japanese ideas for Asian economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, including some good ones, have crashed one after another. This indicates that Tokyo may have problems with diplomacy." \par \par \uc2  "In fact, things got more complicated for Japan as the Asian crisis transformed itself from being purely economic to including issues involving regional security," Kwan added. \par \par \uc2  Washington in January dispatched Defence Secretary William Cohen to the region, and U.S. officials have said repeatedly that the United States has critical economic and national security interests at stake in promoting the restoration of financial stability in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Japanese policy-makers have made clear that they want to contribute more ideas towards global financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. This is in part because further commitment to foreign aid is becoming difficult under severe budgetary constraints, although Japan is still the world's largest provider. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN PAYS, AMERICA SAVES \par \par \uc2  Japan's foreign aid budget is due to be reduced by about 10 percent in the fiscal year starting on April 1. \par \par \uc2  Traditionally, Japan has agreed to foot the bill, while the U.S. has plotted how to save nations. \par \par \uc2  Kwan said: "Given the fact that Japanese financial institutions have loaned six times more than U.S. banks, Japan should be more aggressive in presenting ideas for the regional stability." And the United States needs to contribute not just opinions but money as well, Kwan added. \par \par \uc2  According to the Bank for International Settlements (BIS), Japanese banks' total exposure to Asian countries was $276.2 billion as of the end of June 1997, compared with U.S. banks' total exposure at that time of $46.4 billion. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the global community must come up with rules for burden sharing to form the core of any new order. \par \par \uc2  "In order to discuss how to split the bill among borrowers, lenders, countries, the Group of Seven and international bodies, a permanent institution such as an Asian BIS would be helpful, rather than proceeding on an ad hoc basis," said Masao Suzaki, professor of economics at Tokyo's Senshu University. \par \par \uc2  A concerted, organised approach is essential, rather than just \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or ad hoc meetings, he said. \par \par \uc2  Bernie Fraser, former governor of the Reserve Bank of Australia, who was the first to call for an Asian BIS in 1995, said: "Central banks have more freedom to act in their particular spheres of responsibility, and can usually move more speedily than other organs of government when they need to." \par \par \uc2  Last November, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the value of the Indonesia rupiah in cooperation with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore and Bank Indonesia. But due to a lack of a burden-sharing organisation, the BOJ suffered huge \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses after it bought rupiah at around 3,000 per dollar. The rupiah fell in January to as low as 17,000 per dollar. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3r08jnj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
