\par\par \b \uc2  =Hedge Funds/U.S. Tsys -2: No Signs Of Japanese Sales Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 20:35\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Rumblings of possible yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ surfaced early Wednesday, when traders saw a large player selling Treasurys for about 15 to 20 minutes. Some $2 billion to $3 billion in two-year notes were sold, along with $1 billion of three- and five-year notes, said a trader who knew of the deal. Bond futures soon sold off shortly thereafter, breaching support of 120-20. \par \par \uc2  Although none of the rumors swirling around the market have been confirmed or are expected to be, market participants are cashing in on a long-standing fear: that Japanese investors will be forced to unload Treasurys holdings given the sour state of the economy. "I suspect the rumors reflect more nervousness than anything else," said Frederick Sturm, economist at Fuji Securities. \par \par \uc2  Traders have much to be nervous about. \par \par \uc2  With a new, outspoken governor at the helm, the risk of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the part of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be ruled out. Meanwhile, the contents of the next fiscal stimulus package have yet to be released. And, traders still remember Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's last visit to New York in June, when he sent U.S. markets reeling after he suggested Treasurys sales are a viable possibility in the absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. \par \par \uc2  Since his last visit, Japan's economy has fallen further into the hole, taking the yen with it and suggesting that drastic measures will be necessary to get the country out of its slump. The combination of uncertainties makes good fodder for rumors in the market, meanwhile. Wednesday's barrage of rumors was no exception, either. \par \par \uc2  "There are rumors that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  tonight in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which have turned into rumors the it's the BOJ selling Treasurys (now) to raise dollars so they can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  Others attributed the pullback to anticipation for yet another effort by the Japanese government to fail. \par \par \uc2  "The market is starting to price in the expectation that there's going to be no income tax cut in the stimulus package," said Takanobu Igarashi, economist at Sanwa Bank in New York. Igarashi said that even if the fiscal stimulus package exceeds Y10 trillion, without a tax cut, it would be viewed as "disappointing" to the market. \par \par \uc2  In Thursday's morning edition, Japan's daily Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported that the Japanese government and the ruling LDP had agreed on at least a fiscal stimulus package of at least Y12 trillion. The package didn't include tax cuts and was expected to be financed through a supplementary budget for fiscal 1998, the report said, quoted unnamed sources from the LDP. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think this package will in any way influence (Japanese investor's) decision (to buy or sell Treasurys)," Moran of Daiwa cautioned. \par \par \uc2  By Margaret Boitano 201 938-2046 MBoitano@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  03-25-98 \par \par \uc2  1435EST \par \par \uc2  4228 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du3p00rio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
