\par\par \b \uc2  =Hedge Funds Get Jump On Feared Japanese Sales of U.S. Tsys \b0\par\par\uc2  By Margaret M. Boitano \par \uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 20:29\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--U.S. hedge funds got a head start on selling Treasurys early Wednesday when rumors made the rounds that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was readying for substantial yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the government announces a new fiscal stimulus package this week. \par \par \uc2  The package, which is expected to be unveiled Thursday, is anticipated to fall short of market expectations - something that could send Japanese markets reeling. So some U.S. players decided to get in on the action early by selling Treasurys and the dollar on speculation that Japanese investors would soon follow suit. \par \par \uc2  With less than a week before Japanese corporations close their books for Japan's fiscal year-end, it may be necessary for some to sell Treasurys to book profits. \par \par \uc2  Though traders in New York said they hadn't seen any Japanese sales to speak of so far, that could change should the dollar fall sharply against the yen overnight. \par \par \uc2  "We really haven't seen pronounced selling by Japanese investors, but they certainly have slowed investment," noted Michael Moran, chief economist at Daiwa Securities America Inc. in New York. "They're holding to the sidelines until the end of their fiscal year," he said, adding that he expects buying to pick up again in April. \par \par \uc2  Rumors of possible BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have already sent the dollar below key support at Y130. At about 2:30 p.m. EST, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at Y128.82, compared with Y130.11 in Tokyo earlier and well below Y130.28 in New York late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasurys haven't fared much better. The benchmark 30-year bond had shed 25/32 to trade at 102 21/32 for a yield of 5.933%. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  03-25-98 \par \par \uc2  1429EST \par \par \uc2  4228 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du3p00ri7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
