\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo Midday: Dlr Back In Mid-Y130's; Mkt Rests After BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 03:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar recovered somewhat after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen earlier Friday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains lower than its levels earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were convinced that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market after comments from 'Mr. Yen' - Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara - started to send the dollar down after warning that Japan would take 'decisive measures' to combat the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara told a group of reporters he had 'grave concerns' about the yen's weakness, adding that authorities are prepared to take decisive measures to combat the yen's fall. He repeated those comments in an interview with Dow Jones, but declined to either confirm or deny that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started selling dollars from around Y130.80 through the electronic broking system. \par \par \uc2  'The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the interbank trading system,' said a trader at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  After plunging from an intraday high of Y131.23 around 0005 GMT (7:05 p.m. EST) to a low of Y129.30 at 0047 GMT, the dollar recovered above the Y130-level, and activity subsequently cooled off. \par \par \uc2  'I think we've seen most of the action for the day,' the Japanese bank trader said. 'Everyone is exhausted.' \par \par \uc2  While the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the market wary of taking the dollar up in the near term, weak Japanese fundamentals will keep the yen from rising either. \par \par \uc2  'Right now this is a 'jawboning' market rather than a 'fundamentals' market,' another Japanese bank trader said. 'But the mid-term target is for the dollar to rise.' \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT, the dollar is trading at Y130.10, way down from Y131.15 around 0020 GMT and below Y130.69 in New York late Thursday. It was at Y130.10 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also trading at DM1.8310, down from DM1.8325 earlier in Tokyo and DM1.8325 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at DM1.8280 in Tokyo late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 20-03-98 \par \par \uc2  0236GMT \par \par \uc2  1074 1863 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du3k02ea8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
