\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS-Dlr/yen vol firm, EMS vols see rare action. \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 20 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities in dollar/yen options edged up after choppy trading following spot's rally to a two-month high, while vols in some European crosses gained amid speculation of possible revaluations. \par \par \uc2  Spot dollar/yen had calmed down by the European midsession, but options volatilities stood their ground following an overnight session in which Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the dollar down by about two percent from its two-month peak. \par \par \uc2  "In the short dates like one-and two-weeks, the market is a bit cautious about downside potential, and dollar puts are quite expensive now," said one options trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen vols were quoted at 13.00/13.30 percent at 1130 GMT, up from 12.80/13.10 seen in late European trade on Thursday. One-week had risen to 13.00/14.00 percent from 12.30/13.80 yesterday. \par \par \uc2  Spot dollar stood at 129.90/00, off its overnight peak of 131.30 but up from the low of 129.00. It was at 130.33/43 in late Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the Italian lira and Finnish markka might be revalued were nudging up volatilities in these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the market nervous ahead of a meeting of EU finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Britain this weekend to discuss the euro and other policy issues. \par \par \uc2  Moves last week to put the Greek drachma into the European exchange mechanism after a 14-percent devaluation and the Irish punt's three-percent revaluation were adding to the market's speculation that other EMU candidate countries may be planning similar moves. \par \par \uc2  Italy's Treasury Minister Carlo Azeglio Ciampi on Thursday denied Italy had any plans to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Mark/lira vols have been well bid this morning with people buying the short dates, and the same goes for mark/markka," said the U.S. trader. "The levels are not very high, but it's quite a big move considering where they were before." \par \par \uc2  One-week mark/lira vols were quoted at 2.50/3.50 percent, one-month at 1.70/2.40 percent and one-year at 0.70/1.40 percent. Late on Thursday, vols had been around 0.70/1.50 across the curve. \par \par \uc2  Traders said such moves showed the market expected any euro-zone revaluation to result in a short burst of action, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  migrated to its new ERM parity rate, and then stagnation until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was merged into the euro. \par \par \uc2  Spot mark/lira, currently at 983.22/33, is a little less than one percent away from its parity rate of 990. \par \par \uc2  The story was similar in the Finnish markka. Vols which had been at 0.6 percent across the curve on Thursday had risen to 1.4 percent in the one-month and 0.85 percent in the one-year on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The current spot rate of 3.0300/30 markka per mark is about 0.4 percent away from the central rate of 3.04. \par \par \uc2  Volatilities in sterling/mark, meanwhile, lost some ground as spot stayed firm without breaking into new territory. Spot sterling was at 3.0465/75 marks, steady against 3.0470/80 seen in late Europe on Thursday and off the seven-month high of 3.0565 reached earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling/mark vols will probably trade a little bit lower unless there is a convincing break (in spot) above 3.05, then we could see vols pick up a bit," said a trader at the FINEX futures and options exchange in Dublin. \par \par \uc2  One-month vols had dropped to 7.35/7.75 percent from late Thursday's 7.70/8.10. But the pricing differential between calls and puts showed the market still preferred sterling calls, with 25-delta risk reversals unchanged from Thursday at 0.05/0.35. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6721, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3k092om\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
