\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar battles to maintain firm footing vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 17 (Reuters) - The dollar battled to maintain a firm footing against the yen as concern about Japan's economic outlook and financial industry was pitched against wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and talk of income tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  Expectations that Japanese stimulus measures will not do enough, quickly enough, to kick start the country's stagnant economy helped to drive the dollar to a two-month high of 129.76 yen on Monday. \par \par \uc2  While the yen has clawed back ground against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since then, analysts do not expect it to stave off renewed weakness for much longer. \par \par \uc2  "The general background in Japan is very negative for the yen but we have had a temporary adjustment after we almost got through the magical 130 level in New York," said Gerard Lyons, chief economist at DKB International in London. \par \par \uc2  "The trigger for the pullback was talk of income tax cuts but I expect the yen to weaken." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 129.24/29 yen at 0944 GMT compared with an Asian high of 129.70 and 129.40/45 at the close of European trading on Monday. Mark/yen was at 71.10/12 from 71.16/19 late yesterday after being forced back overnight from technical resistance between 71.25-35. \par \par \uc2  The yen was offered some respite overnight after Taku Yamasaki, head of the Japanese ruling Liberal Democratic Party's Policy Research Council, was quoted as saying the party was considering a cut in the maximum income tax rate. \par \par \uc2  Speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar from rallying beyond 130 yen in the run up to the end of the Japanese fiscal year on March 31 also underpinned the yen in Asia on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "There was talk of BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight and while there was no confirmation, the fear will help cap the dollar," said Sonja Gibbs, strategist at Nomura Research International. \par \par \uc2  Traders also speculated that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be more active in supporting the yen after Masaru Hayami takes over as Bank of Japan Governor from Yasuo Matsushita, who is to resign on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the dollar was trapped against the mark as concern about allegations of sexual impropriety made against U.S. President Bill Clinton ebbed for the time being, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The market has used the Clinton sex scandal as an excuse to take a bit of profit but there are no major developments expected to shake dollar/mark out of its range," said Ernesto Ramirez, international economist at Bankers Trust. \par \par \uc2  February U.S. housing starts data out at 1330 GMT, and industrial production and capacity utilisation statistics for the same month due at 1415 GMT may give the dollar direction. It is, however, more likely to be whipped around by swings in mark crosses, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Gibbs at Nomura Research International said the dollar was particularly open to be influenced by sterling's reaction to the British budget which will be unveiled later today. \par \par \uc2  The pound will fall if Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown presents a tighter-than-expected budget which reduces the need for further British interest rate increases, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  For the time being, however, "the broad sentiment for the pound is very positive," said Gibbs. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at 3.0275/80 marks and 1.6652/62 at 0905 GMT from 3.0365/75 and 1.6675/85 late in Europe on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere the Greek drachma and the Irish punt remained at the top of the European exchange-rate mechanism, trading 2.90 percent and 1.01 percent stronger than their respective central parities against the mark. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du3h000bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
