\par\par \b \uc2  BOE says too soon to push Japan on stimulus -- Jiji. \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 27 (Reuters) - Bank of England Governor Eddie George said it is too early to pressure Japan into crafting a supplementary budget to stimulate its economy, Japan's Jiji news agency said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  In a report from London, Jiji quoted George as saying in a recent interview that at a meeting last weekend in London not all the rest of the Group of Seven (G7) nations' finance ministers and central bankers urged Tokyo to take fiscal steps to boost its economy. \par \par \uc2  George welcomed the Japanese government's plan to use up to 13 trillion yen to stabilise the financial system through the strengthening of banks' capital bases, saying that the government has taken a big step forward in deciding to use public money. \par \par \uc2  If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  too freely to save every institution, that could give rise to moral hazard, George said, adding that he did not believe this was a serious risk in Japan at the moment. \par \par \uc2  He said the International Monetary Fund (IMF) is aware the Asian financial crisis is unprecedented, but calls for the IMF to provide as much money to Asian countries as they need is unrealistic. \par \par \uc2  George said it would be too simplistic to suggest Japan throw money to Asia if the IMF does not do so. \par \par \uc2  But Japan is likely to face difficulties in using tax money for the provision of international aid, as it already faced heavy criticism over the use of public funds just to help save its financial institutions, George was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's proposal to introduce a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system may have a role in the long term, but in the short term, it could be risky, George said. \par \par \uc2  The Asian economic turmoil could push down global economic growth by around one percentage point, but the impact from Asia can be contained if demand in Europe and North America continues to expand, he said. \par \par \uc2  George also said a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to be established with the introduction of European economic and monetary union, is likely to base its money supply policy on the Bundesbank's. \par \par \uc2  The Jiji interview, published in Japanese, was translated back into English by Reuters. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2r00zfp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
