\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch:Some Officials Rue Missed Chances To Raise Rates \b0\par\par\uc2  By Takeshi Takeuchi \par \uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1998\par 09:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Faced with an increasingly uphill battle to prop up the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, some middle-ranking Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials rue the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's failure to lift interest rates early last spring - a step that might have supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in subsequent months. \par \par \uc2  'We probably missed the right timing for raising the official discount rate (ODR),' sighed a mid-level Bank of Japan official earlier this week. His comment came after the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar from rising to Y135 by selling dollars in the low Y134.00 range - a step that has failed to quell dollar-bullish sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has kept the ODR at a historic low of 0.50% since September 1995. Low interest rates in Japan have contributed to the growing disinterest in yen-denominated assets, creating a 'sell Japan' mood. \par \par \uc2  'A slightly higher interest rate wouldn't solve the problem, I know. But still, if we could have raised the interest rate by 0.5 or 1.0 percentage point early last spring, it might have had the effect of lending some support to the weak yen now,' said this official. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 09-01-98 \par \par \uc2  0853GMT \par \par \uc2  4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1900798\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
