\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr up in Tokyo on view U.S. may not help on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  0643 GMT\tab     TRADED HIGH/LOW AS OF 0600 GMT\par  USD/JPY     132.98/08\tab \tab 132.62-131.27\par  USD/DEM     1.8281/86\tab \tab 1.8278-1.8205\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar resumed its rise against the yen in late Tokyo after a U.S. newspaper report fuelled speculation that the U.S. Treasury may be unwilling to coordinate with Japan in its effort to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The report offset earlier comments by Japan's Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara that Washington and Tokyo had agreed to continue cooperating on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The United States and Japan will continue to cooperate as always, including in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Sakakibara told reporters after a dinner meeting with U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the talks had broached the issue of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G7 member nations, he said, "No comment." \par \par \uc2  In contrast, an early Thursday edition of Washington Post said that Treasury officials who met senior Japanese officials on Wednesday "made no effort to dispel the view that they are reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the absence of major new initiatives by the Japanese". \par \par \uc2  "People thought what the Washington Post said was reasonable enough to rely on," said a dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an earlier peak of 132.62 yen in morning trade after Sakakibara declined to comment on the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after he met Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  But the gains were later wiped out by a wave of dollar sales, mainly by overseas funds and mark/yen sales by German operators. Speculation that Japan might take further steps, such as corporate tax cuts, to stimulate its economy in return for joint action to stem the yen's slide boosted Japan's stock market in the morning and also weighed on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But a majority of operators later changed their bets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The United States and others won't agree on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to abandon its fiscal austerity policy and, say, implements huge tax cuts," said Yuhiko Kaji, senior analyst at Sakura Bank. "Without such policy changes, joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be effective, but they would be too risky for (Prime Minister Ryutaro) Hashimoto's government now." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara visits New York on Thursday to meet commercial bankers and New York Fed President William McDonough. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumoured to have bought the dollar during its late-afternoon rally above 132 yen and European operators joined in, buying both the dollar and mark against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Continued falls in some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also supportive for the dollar, dealers said. The Indonesian rupiah slumped to a record low of 9,900 to the dollar on Thursday against Wednesday's low of around 8,450. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said one remaining hope for yen bulls was what Japanese Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary Fukushiro Nukaga, now in Washington, would say after meeting Summers on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We are wondering why Mr Nukaga is there before the parliamentary session starts next week," said a dealer at a city bank. "There is a slight hope that he might have some present to make the U.S. side happy, for example, extra measures to boost Japan's fragile economy." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said even if the chance of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined, there remains the possibility the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone if the dollar rises above 134 yen. The BOJ conducted dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 134 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 131.55/65 yen and 1.8236/42 marks in New York on Wednesday. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +813 3 432-8570 fax +813 3 432-7462, tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du18019no\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
