\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Attempts To Support Yen Through  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Fowler \par \uc2  Staff Reporter \par \uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par 13:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Traders in Tokyo proved once again Tuesday that you can't keep a good dollar down, as they bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's attempts to support the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Market participants said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its partners at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to fight a losing battle in trying to cap the dollar's rise against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamentals of the U.S. economy are so strong that no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can stop the dollar's rise," said Koichi Kubo, assistant manger of the international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  group at Dai-ichi Kangyo Bank. \par \par \uc2  Weak fundamentals in Japan, ongoing turmoil in Asian markets and steady economic growth in the U.S. will probably encourage traders to take advantage of every dip in the dollar to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan entered the market early in the Tokyo session, just after the dollar had strengthened to 134.30 yen, its highest level since April 27, 1992, when it touched an intraday high of 134.95 yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan dollar sales, which were confirmed by a Japanese monetary source on condition of anonymity, initially knocked the dollar more than one yen lower. But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  clawed its way back to end Asian trading at 133.65 yen, up from 133.58 yen late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  It was the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-December, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold between $8 billion and $10 billion over a three-day period to push the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower from 131.50 yen to below 127.00 yen temporarily. \par \par \uc2  The action Tuesday followed repeated warnings in the last few weeks by Japanese monetary authorities regarding the dollar's climb against the yen. Ten days ago, Eisuke Sakakibara, vice minister for international affairs at the finance ministry, warned \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a surprising scale" if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Following the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in Tokyo, said, "It looks like Japanese officials are trying to put some bite into their words." \par \par \uc2  Most traders said the bite isn't as big as the bark in the case of Japanese monetary authorities. Most market watchers bet the dollar will shrug off such efforts and test 135.00 yen soon, perhaps as early as this week. \par \par \uc2  "They could do a big \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling dollars and buying yen. But if that pushes interest rates higher, it probably wouldn't be good for the economy or for stocks," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is kind of a last resort for everyone," said Satoshi Inoue, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  look very risky and Japan is not so attractive, so people are buying dollars every chance they get," Inoue said, adding that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain buoyant until the summer. \par \par \uc2  Some said Japanese authorities probably aren't aiming to do anything more than slow the pace of the dollar's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ look to us like a makeshift measure to buy time until the Japanese government can come with another substantial package to stimulate the economy," said Katsuaki Tanaka, a trader at Nippon Credit Bank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also doubt the Japanese will be able to gather any sort of joint effort by the Group of Seven countries to support the yen, although some have speculated that a visit by Sakakibara to London Tuesday and Washington Wednesday could be aimed at building support for such steps. Member nations of the Group of Seven countries are the U.S., Canada, Japan, the United Kingdom, Germany, Italy and France. \par \par \uc2  "It would make a world of difference if the U.S. Treasury were to join in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I don't think it will because that would undermine confidence in financial markets," Landon said. "A shift in dollar policy at this point would risk pushing the economy into recession, rates would rise...just ahead of (U.S.) congressional elections in November," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's finance ministry also seems to be concerned that the weakening of the yen will drive foreign investors further from Tokyo's stock market, a development that would exacerbate problems weighing on the economy, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2  1138 4204 \