\par\par \b \uc2  Joint action needed to halt yen's fall--traders. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 1998\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major industrial nations would be needed to stop the mighty dollar from powering higher against the yen, but key allies may have yet to reach agreement on such a move, traders say. \par \par \uc2  They say the greenback could rocket as high as 140 yen within a month's time in the absence of concerted action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has grown, and such a step could be needed to pre-empt the greater risk of the dollar's reversal over the long term," said Kazunori Iida, manager of the financial products department at Nissho Iwai Corp. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the greenback continued to rise against the yen despite repeated attempts by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to staunch the drive through dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's failure to dent the dollar has led to a perception that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone is proving to be futile. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to sell dollars in the morning in the wake of the dollar's climb above 134.38 yen, its highest level since April 1992, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a series of comments by Japanese government officials expressing worries about the weak yen sent the dollar as low as 132.60 yen before it began to recover. \par \par \uc2  Lingering uncertainty over the state of the Japanese economy kept yen buyers at bay, with the dollar recovering close to 134 yen. It was standing at 133.20 yen in late afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investors are shifting their assets into U.S. dollars and dollar-based instruments by the truckload due to the dollar's status as a "safe haven" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid economic turmoil in Asia and underperforming markets elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  Nissho Iwai's Iida said the dollar's strong performance could pose the risk of deflation in the United States and widen Japan's trade surplus with other nations, which would in turn put the dollar at risk of falling steeply in the long run. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan implied on Monday that the United States could face deflation and that could cause difficulties for the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Iida said: "If concerted action took place, I think the dollar could tumble by at least by five yen." \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a European bank in Tokyo said there is a feeling that concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take place if the dollar rises above 135 yen. \par \par \uc2  Overseas operators were buying short-dated, or near-term, dollar put options to hedge against possible joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though they were buying dollars on the spot market, he said. A put is an option to sell at a certain price by a specified date. \par \par \uc2  An overseas trip by influential Finance Ministry official Eisuke Sakakibara this week is raising speculation that he may ask other nations for concerted action to stem the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara is visiting London and the United States to explain to government officials measures that Japan has recently announced to stabilise the financial system. \par \par \uc2  Traders say it is unclear whether Japan will get cooperation from other nations even if it asks for it. \par \par \uc2  Sadaaki Tsunakawa, vice president at Citibank, said there was no compelling reason for the United States to join Japan in containing the rising dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The only reason for such action may be to stop the 'sell Japan' trend, but pushing the dollar down against the yen would not be the answer," Tsunakawa added. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin reiterated on Monday that the United States wanted a strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said that in order for Japan to gain the support of other nations, it might need to come up with drastic steps to stimulate its economy such as larger cuts income tax and speeding up economic deregulation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say if concerted action does not occur, the dollar is likely to climb to 138 to 140 yen within a month. \par \par \uc2  "If the government takes further drastic steps, concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible. Otherwise the dollar's rise may not come to an end soon, partly because economic problems in Asia are much deeper than initially anticipated," said Ko Haruki, chief dealer at Midland Bank's Tokyo branch. \par \par \uc2  Japan is not battling alone in Asia to stem the dollar's relentless rise. Other Asian nations saw their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit record lows against the dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah fell to a low of 7,600 to the dollar, while the Thai baht plunged to a low of 52.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk 81-3-3432-1396 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du160164m\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql 